    <details class="bcv2-section" id="instructions">
      <summary>
        <span class="bcv2-section-title">Instructions</span>
        <span class="bcv2-section-dek">Got one of my cards? Heres how to use it.</span>
      </summary>

      <p>So you got one of my business cards, awesome! Its also a tiny USB PD power supply, here's how to use it.</p>
      <p>Want to see everything its doing? Plug it into your computer and open the <a href="/bcv2/console/">console</a>.</p>
      <p><em>This page is a work in progress, so check back again soon!</em></p>

      <h3>What can it do?</h3>
      <h4>See what your charger offers</h4>
      <p>Plug it into a USB-C charger and the {{ silk("SOURCE CAPABILITY") }} bar lights up every voltage it can give you, from 5 to 48V. The mode lights show which PD modes it supports (PDO, PPS, SAVS and EAVS), the one its using is brighter. In a few seconds you know if that charger is any good.</p>
      <h4>Power your projects</h4>
      <p>Pick a voltage, press {{ key("APPLY") }} and the card negotiates it with the charger. Then press {{ key("LOAD ON/OFF") }} to send it to your load. A very slim and portable power supply, all you need is a PD charger and a cable!</p>

      <h3>How to use it</h3>
      <div class="bcv2-steps">
        <h4>Plug it in</h4>
        <p>Plug a USB PD charger into the {{ silk("POWER") }} port. The displays show the voltage, current and power going out, and the bar shows what the charger offers.</p>

        <h4>Hook up your load</h4>
        <p>Use the {{ silk("WIRE WEDGE") }}, banana plugs, or the 0.1" headers. Wedge the wire in like below, or just clip a crocodile clip on it. Keep your load's capacitance under 90uF, its printed on the card too.</p>
        <figure class="bcv2-figure">
          <img src="{{ img }}wire-wedge-700.webp" srcset="{{ img }}wire-wedge-700.webp 700w, {{ img }}wire-wedge-1400.webp 1400w" sizes="(max-width: 860px) calc(100vw - 40px), 820px" width="1400" height="1019" alt="A crocodile clip on the top bar of the wire wedge and a wire wedged under the bottom bar, pulled along the board" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
          <figcaption>A crocodile clip on top, a wedged wire on the bottom.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h4>Change the voltage or current</h4>
        <p>Press {{ key("V/A") }} to choose voltage or current, then {{ key("FINE +") }} and {{ key("FINE −") }} for small steps or {{ key("COARSE +") }} and {{ key("COARSE −") }} for big ones. The new value blinks until you press {{ key("APPLY") }}.</p>
        <p>Dont want to press {{ key("APPLY") }} every time? hold it for {{ silk("AUTO APPLY") }}, now every change goes through right away. Hold it again to turn it off.</p>

        <h4>Pick a mode</h4>
        <p>{{ key("MODE") }} picks between AUTO, PDO, PPS, SAVS and EAVS. Tap it and the one you're on blinks, tap again to move to the next one, then press {{ key("APPLY") }} (or just wait 5 seconds). AUTO stays lit dimly while its on.</p>
        <p>If the new mode cant do your voltage, the card goes to the next one down. If it has nothing lower, it goes up to the lowest one it has: your old voltage flashes fast as a warning, and the load turns off before it changes. Want to go up with the load still on? Pick the higher voltage in AUTO.</p>
        <p>Hold {{ key("MODE") }} to turn EPR on or off, that's where 28V and up live.</p>

        <h4>Turn the output on</h4>
        <p>{{ key("LOAD ON/OFF") }} turns your output on and off, and {{ silk("LOAD EN") }} lights up when its on. If {{ silk("LOAD EN") }} blinks, the output cant turn on yet, or the change you're making will turn it off when you apply it.</p>

        <h4>Lock the buttons</h4>
        <p>Hold {{ key("V/A") }} for a second so nothing changes accidentally, and hold it again to unlock. {{ key("LOAD ON/OFF") }} still works while its locked.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="bcv2-panel">
        <h3>What the lights mean</h3>
        <p>{{ silk("THERM LIM") }} means the card is dimming its display to keep its regulator happy, which happens at higher voltages. Nothing is wrong.</p>
        <p>{{ silk("PPS CV") }} and {{ silk("PPS CL") }} glow dimly whenever you're on PPS. The bright one shows if the charger is holding your voltage (CV) or limiting your current (CL).</p>
        <p>If {{ silk("WARNING") }} blinks, its spelling a letter in Morse code:</p>
        <ul class="bcv2-morse">
          <li><strong>R</strong>{{ morse(".-.") }}<span>the charger said no, or it said yes but never gave the voltage (the card tries a few times first).</span></li>
          <li><strong>S</strong>{{ morse("...") }}<span>your settings couldnt be saved.</span></li>
          <li><strong>T</strong>{{ morse("-") }}<span>the power dropped out.</span></li>
          <li><strong>F</strong>{{ morse("..-.") }}<span>the card lost power a few times while restoring your last setting, so it started with a safer one. Press {{ key("APPLY") }} to try again.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>Chargers are all different</h3>
      <p>Not every charger offers every mode, and some just dont play nice with it. If something doesnt work or acts weird, tell you what, try another cable first - then another charger.</p>
      <div class="bcv2-tips">
        <div class="bcv2-tip">
          <h4>Only seeing 5V?</h4>
          <p>Then you arent using a full PD power supply, so thats all the card can get. Use a USB PD charger or powerbank thats atleast 20W, laptop chargers are usually the best ones.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bcv2-tip">
          <h4>Want 28V and up?</h4>
          <p>You'll need an EPR charger (these are usually 140W or more) and a 240W cable.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The console</h3>
      <h4>Connect</h4>
      <p>The card has a second USB port, {{ silk("DATA") }}, and it only carries data. Keep your charger in {{ silk("POWER") }}, plug {{ silk("DATA") }} into your computer, then open <a href="/bcv2/console/">anasmalas.com/bcv2/console</a> in Chrome or Edge and press Connect. It works in Chrome on Android too.</p>
      <h4>What it does</h4>
      <p>You can see everything your charger offers in detail, request exact voltages, and watch live graphs of voltage, current and power. It also tells you if theres newer firmware for your card and how to update it.</p>
      <p>If your card restarts on its own, the console reads its black box when it reconnects. Thats a small log of what went wrong.</p>
      <h4>Bug reports</h4>
      <p>Bug reports are on by default. When something goes wrong, like the card restarting, the charger saying yes but not giving you the voltage, or the console itself breaking, I get the black box and the log so I can look into it and fix it in the next firmware. It really helps!</p>
      <p>Dont want that? Hit "Turn off" on the little notice at the top, or untick "Share anonymous bug reports" any time.</p>
      <p>You can also press "Report a bug" any time and tell me what happened. Or save the report and send it to the email on the card, scan the QR code on the card to add me to your phone contacts quickly :) Emailing it isnt anonymous of course, but the report still has your card's ID removed.</p>

      <h3 id="update">Updating the firmware</h3>
      <p class="bcv2-callout">Head to <a href="/bcv2/update/">anasmalas.com/bcv2/update</a>, it shows you the easiest way for whatever you're on. On a Mac or an Android phone it updates right in the browser, nothing to install. The console tells you when theres a new one.</p>

      <h3 id="privacy">Bug reports and your data</h3>
      <h4>What gets sent</h4>
      <p>Only when something goes wrong, or when you send a report yourself. Turn bug reports off and nothing gets sent unless you hit Send.</p>
      <p>A report has the firmware version, your browser and system (like Chrome 140 on Windows), what your charger offers, the last minute of voltage and current readings, the console log, the black box, any errors from the console itself, and whatever you wrote.</p>
      <p>Reports go to this site (anasmalas.com, which runs on Cloudflare) and get deleted after 180 days.</p>
      <h4>What doesnt</h4>
      <p>Your card's ID is removed before anything is sent, and your IP address isnt stored. No cookies either, your browser just remembers if you turned reports off. Please note that I'll use an LLM to process this data, so please dont write anything youre not willing to share with it. The only other thing the console asks this site for is the latest firmware version.</p>
    </details>

    <p class="bcv2-signoff">Have fun with it, and tag me when you make use of it for something cool! :)</p>
